# Oh my darling Clementine - Amerikaanse volksballade
# Oh My Darling, Clementine is een Amerikaanse ballade; de tekst wordt meestal
# toegeschreven aan Percy Montrose (1884), de herkomst van de wijs is onbekend
# (Wikipedia, en; de Morgan Library vermeldt een eerste uitgave bij Oliver Ditson, ca. 1884).
# Publiek domein.
# Overgenomen uit DARLINGC.LEM (WordPerfect 5.1, 1991), waar na twee Nederlandse versies de Engelse
# staat. In de bron is een akkoord met de tekstregel eronder samengevloeid; hier
# gesplitst. Tikfouten hersteld: "Rubby", "allas", "Thoufgt", "Tough" en "mis".
# Akkoorden bij het eerste couplet en het refrein, naar de kolommen van de bron.
{title: Oh my darling Clementine}
{meta: id oh-my-darling-clementine}
{meta: language en}
{lyricist: Percy Montrose}
{composer: traditioneel}
{year: 1884}
{copyright: Public domain}
{meta: license public-domain}
{meta: source Overgenomen uit DARLINGC.LEM (WordPerfect 5.1, 1991), Engelse versie; herkomst via Wikipedia (en) en de Morgan Library}
{meta: tag kampvuur}
{meta: tag volkslied}
{meta: variant-group oh-my-darling-clementine}
{key: F}

{start_of_verse}
In a [F]cavern in a canyon
excavating for [Gm]a mine
lived a [C7]miner forty [F]niner
and [C7]his daughter Clemen[F]tine.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
Oh my [F]darling, oh my darling
Oh my darling Clemen[Gm]tine
You are [C7]lost for gone [F]for ever
dreadful [C7]sorry Clemen[F]tine.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse}
Light she was and like a fairy
And her shoes were number nine,
Herring boxes, without topses,
Sandals were for Clementine.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse}
Drove she duckling to the water,
Every morning just at nine.
Stubbed her toe upon a splinter.
Fell into the foaming brine.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse}
Ruby lips above the water,
Blowing bubbles, soft and fine,
But alas I was no swimmer,
So, I lost my Clementine.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse}
There's a churchyard on the hillside
Where the flowers grow and twine,
There grow roses, 'mongst the posies,
Fertilized by Clementine.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse}
Then the miner Forty-niner
soon began to peak and pine
Thought he "oughter fine" his daughter
now he is with Clementine
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse}
In My dreams she still doth haunt me
robed in garment soaked in brine
Though in life I used to hug her
now she's dead I'll draw the line
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse}
How I miss her, how I miss her
how I miss my Clementine
Now I kiss her little sister
and forget my Clementine
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}
